<item>
  <title>An Introduction to Variance</title>
  <dc:creator>Your Name</dc:creator>
  <link>https://your-website-url.example.com/posts/01-variance-intro/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>Welcome to the blog! Many students find statistics intimidating, but we are going to break it down step-by-step. Today, we are looking at variance.</p>
<section id="the-intuition" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-intuition">The Intuition</h3>
<p>Variance simply tells us how spread out our data is around the average. If everyone in the class gets exactly a 7.0 on a test, the variance is zero. If grades range from 2.0 to 10.0, the variance is high.</p>
</section>
<section id="the-math" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-math">The Math</h3>
<p>Here is the formal formula for sample variance. Notice how we divide by <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?n-1"> instead of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?n"> to correct for bias:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s%5E2%20=%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5E%7Bn%7D%20(x_i%20-%20%5Cbar%7Bx%7D)%5E2%7D%7Bn-1%7D"></p>
</section>
<section id="the-code" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-code">The Code</h3>
<p>Instead of calculating this by hand, here is how we ask R to do the heavy lifting for a small sample of student grades:</p>
</section>
<section id="a-list-of-student-grades" class="level1">
<h1>A list of student grades</h1>
<p>student_grades &lt;- c(6.5, 7.0, 8.2, 5.5, 9.0)</p>
</section>
<section id="calculate-the-variance" class="level1">
<h1>Calculate the variance</h1>
<p>var(student_grades)</p>


</section>

